          "text": "The pattern indexes as an **orthorhombic primitive cell**, with the systematic absences consistent with **Pnma (No. 62)**. The solution is the **CeMg-type oP16 structure**, containing eight CeMg formula units per cell.\n\nRefined lattice from the principal peak positions:\n\n- \\(a \\approx 8.776\\) \u00c5\n- \\(b \\approx 5.132\\) \u00c5\n- \\(c \\approx 8.940\\) \u00c5\n- \\(Z=8\\)\n\nRepresentative indexing is 14.13\u00b0 (101), 22.38\u00b0 (111), 28.48\u00b0 (202), 32.17\u00b0 (301), 33.49\u00b0 (212), 34.92\u00b0 (020), 36.73\u00b0 (311), 37.85\u00b0 (121), 43.31\u00b0 (303), 45.66\u00b0 (222), 46.95\u00b0 (313), and 48.22\u00b0 (321).\n\nThe lattice and space-group assignment are substantially more certain than the positional parameters. The latter are an approximate chemically regular CeMg-type starting model and should be refined against the full profile.\n\n```cif\ndata_CeMg_Pnma\n_audit_creation_method\n'Indexed from supplied Cu Kalpha1 powder pattern; approximate CeMg-type positional model'\n\n_chemical_name_common                  'cerium magnesium'\n_chemical_formula_sum                  'Ce Mg'\n_chemical_formula_structural           'CeMg'\n_cell_formula_units_Z                  8\n\n_symmetry_space_group_name_H-M         'P n m a'\n_symmetry_Int_Tables_number            62\n_space_group_crystal_system            orthorhombic\n\n_cell_length_a                         8.776\n_cell_length_b                         5.132\n_cell_length_c                         8.940\n_cell_angle_alpha                      90\n_cell_angle_beta                       90\n_cell_angle_gamma                      90\n_cell_volume                           402.6\n\n_diffrn_radiation_type                 'Cu Kalpha1'\n_diffrn_radiation_wavelength           1.5406\n\nloop_\n_space_group_symop_id\n_space_group_symop_operation_xyz\n1  'x,y,z'\n2  '1/2-x,-y,1/2+z'\n3  '-x,1/2+y,-z'\n4  '1/2+x,1/2-y,1/2-z'\n5  '-x,-y,-z'\n6  '1/2+x,y,1/2-z'\n7  'x,1/2-y,z'\n8  '1/2-x,1/2+y,1/2+z'\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\n_atom_site_B_iso_or_equiv\nCe1 Ce 4 c 0.048 0.250 0.143 1.0 0.5\nCe2 Ce 4 c 0.296 0.250 0.404 1.0 0.5\nMg1 Mg 4 c 0.061 0.250 0.654 1.0 0.7\nMg2 Mg 4 c 0.309 0.250 0.892 1.0 0.7\n```\n\nInterchanging \\(a\\) and \\(c\\), together with the corresponding coordinates, gives an equivalent nonstandard setting rather than a distinct structural alternative. A polar **Pna2\u2081** subgroup cannot be ruled out from peak positions alone, but the powder pattern does not require symmetry lowering from Pnma.",
